# check-rls-policies.allow
#
# Allowlist of RESTRICTIVE-by-design tables for scripts/check-rls-policies.sh.
#
# Format:
#   - One bare lowercase table name per line.
#   - '#' starts a comment (whole-line or inline).
#   - Blank lines are ignored.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING AN ENTRY
# ---------------------------------
# The RESTRICTIVE-only-policy pattern looks like a config preference but
# is almost always a real silent-deny bug. We have 4 production fixup
# migrations (0077, 0078, 0329, 0336) on file precisely because devs
# kept assuming "RESTRICTIVE was sufficient on its own." It is NOT.
#
# A table belongs in this allowlist iff ALL of the following hold:
#   1. `postwave_app` is NEVER granted SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on
#      the table (the role would always be RLS-blocked anyway).
#   2. Reads/writes only happen via SECURITY DEFINER functions owned by
#      the postgres superuser (bypasses RLS), background workers using
#      the migration role, or admin tooling that runs as the owner.
#   3. Adding a PERMISSIVE policy would actually WIDEN the trust
#      boundary, not narrow it.
#
# If you cannot honestly write that justification next to the entry,
# the table is a real silent-deny bug. Fix it with a companion
# PERMISSIVE policy using the 0077/0078/0336 canonical pattern.
#
# Each entry MUST carry a justification comment on the same or
# preceding line, citing the migration that introduced the table and
# the worker / function that owns access.
#
# Example (template — uncomment + edit for a real exemption):
#
#   # 9999: only written by the audit-trail SECURITY DEFINER function;
#   # postwave_app has no GRANT on this table at all.
#   # audit_trail_immutable

# -- intentionally empty --
